"(PDF) Combinatorial t-designs from quadratic functions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/334478364_Combinatorial_t-designs_from_quadratic_functions)\nciteturn8search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.3 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.1907.06235 ... In this paper, we investigate this idea further by using quadratic functions over finite fields, thereby obtain infinite families of 2-designs, and explicitly determine their parameters. ... Furthermore, we confirmed Conjecture 3 in Ding and Tang (arXiv: 1903.07375, 2019). ... arXiv:1907.06235v1 [cs.IT] 14 Jul 2019 ... Lenz, Design Theory, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1999.\nPreprint PDF Available\n\n# Combinatorial t-designs from quadratic functions\n\n  * July 2019\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.1907.06235\n\nAuthors:\n\nCan Xiang\n\nCan Xiang\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nXin Ling\n\nXin Ling\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nQi Wang\n\n  * Southern University of Science and Technology\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nCombinatorial t-designs have been an interesting topic in combinatorics for decades. It was recently reported that the image sets of a fixed size of certain special polynomials may constitute a t-design. Till now only a small amount of work on constructing t-designs from special polynomials has been done, and it is in general hard to determine their parameters. In this paper, we investigate this idea further by using quadratic functions over finite fields, thereby obtain infinite families of 2-designs, and explicitly determine their parameters. The obtained designs cover some earlier 2-designs as special cases. Furthermore, we confirmed Conjecture 3 in Ding and Tang (arXiv: 1903.07375, 2019).\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\