of the weighted Catalan numbers is equal to that of the corresponding unweighted ones.\n</visual_element>\n\nWe consider notable variations of the Catalan numbers, namely weighted, bounded, and multidimensional Catalan numbers. For particular weights, the weighted Catalan numbers have many combinatorial interpretations. For example, when the weights are \\(q^x\\), we obtain the \\(q\\)-Catalan numbers [3], which encode the distribution of areas under Dyck paths. Postnikov [4] shows that when the weight function is \\((2k+1)^2\\), the corresponding sequence \\(C_n^b\\) counts combinatorial types of Morse links of order \\(n\\). Postnikov conjectured that \\(C_n^b\\) has a period of \\(2\\cdot 3^{r-3}\\) modulo \\(3^r\\), meaning that \\(2\\cdot 3^{r-3}\\) is the smallest positive integer such that \\(C_{n+2\\cdot 3^{r-3}}^b-C_n^b\\) is a multiple of \\(3^r\\) for large \\(n\\). This was later proven by Gao and Gu [5] in 2021, who also provide a condition for the periodicity of the weighted Catalan numbers. When we count the number of objects of a given type, the divisibility properties of these numbers illuminate patterns or provide ways to construct all the objects efficiently. In 2006, Postnikov and Sagan [6] derived a condition under which the 2-adic valuation of the weighted Catalan numbers is equal to that of the corresponding unweighted ones. Later in the year, Konvalinka [7] proved an analogous result for the \\(q\\)-Catalan numbers. In 2010, An [8] proved a conjecture by Konvalinka and studied other divisibility properties using matrices. Later, in 2012, Shader [9] considered the periodicity modulo \\(p^r\\) for prime \\(p\\) of certain weighted Catalan numbers.\n\nWe build on the above results by extending the definition of weighted two-dimensional Catalan numbers to weighted multidimensional ones by considering a height function of similar behavior. A particular case is when the height is bounded from above. We are interested in seeing what analogous properties hold. We derive a result on the periodicity similar to that of Gao and Gu [5]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Xin Ling (https://dblp1.uni-trier.de/pid/238/0453.html)\nciteturn8search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; CoRR abs/1907.06235 (2019)\nIEEE Trans. Comput. Aided Des. Integr. Circuits Syst. 44(7): 2516-2529 (2025)\n\nZhiyue Liu, Fanrong Ma, Xin Ling:\nTarget-oriented Multimodal Sentiment Classification with Counterfactual-enhanced Debiasing. ICME 2025: 1-6\n\nZhiyue Liu, Fanrong Ma, Xin Ling:\nTarget-oriented Multimodal Sentiment Classification with Counterfactual-enhanced Debiasing. CoRR abs/2509.09160 (2025)\n\nXin Ling, Can Xiang:\nInfinite families of t-designs from the binomial x^{4}+x^{3} over $\\mathrm {GF}(2^n)$. Appl. Algebra Eng. Commun. Comput. 34(3): 411-421 (2023)\n\nXin Ling, Sihem Mesnager, Yanfeng Qi, Chunming TangImage: 0000-0001-9599-851X:\nA class of narrow-sense BCH codes over $\\mathbb {F}_q$ of length $\\frac{q^m-1}{2}$. Des. Codes Cryptogr. 88(2): 413-427 (2020)\n\nCan Xiang, Xin Ling, Qi WangImage: 0000-0001-9780-5443:\nCombinatorial t-designs from quadratic functions. Des. Codes Cryptogr. 88(3): 553-565 (2020)\n\nXin Ling, Sihem Mesnager, Yanfeng Qi, Chunming Tang:\nA class of narrow-sense BCH codes over F_{q} of length (q^{m}-1)/2. CoRR abs/1903.06779 (2019)\n\nCan Xiang, Xin Ling, Qi Wang:\nCombinatorial t-designs from quadratic functions. CoRR abs/1907.06235 (2019)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Qi Wang 0012 (https://dblp.org/pid/19/1924-12.html)\nciteturn8search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week;       * journals/corr/abs-1907-06235 ...       * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-1907-06235\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-1907-06235\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n  